{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Whatever parts the hoof and is cloven-footed and brings up the cud</b> — The reason of this verse is that any animal that has both of these signs you may eat, but you may not eat [an animal with] one of them alone. And it would have been fitting to state this in general terms, except that Scripture specified the camel and the hyrax and the hare regarding the cud, and the pig regarding the hoof, because there are no others in the world that have the one sign alone. And it went back and said (Leviticus 11:8), \"of their flesh you shall not eat,\" as a negative commandment. And Rashi wrote: I know only these; other unclean animals that have no sign of purity at all, from where [do we know it]? You say by a fortiori argument: if these, which have some signs of purity, are prohibited, etc. And the language of the Sifra (chapter 3:2) is: if these, which have signs of purity, are subject to a negative commandment regarding their consumption, should not other unclean animals that have no sign of purity logically be subject to a negative commandment regarding their consumption? Thus the camel and the hyrax and the hare and the pig are derived from the verse, and other unclean animals from a fortiori argument. It follows that the positive commandment concerning them is from the verse, [and] the negative commandment from a fortiori argument. This is the end of the baraita. But in my opinion it does not hold up properly according to the talmudic sugyot, for if so, one would not receive lashes for other unclean animals, which are derived only from a fortiori reasoning, for we do not issue warnings from logical inference. And thus they said regarding \"his sister\": Scripture prohibited his paternal sister or his maternal sister (Leviticus 20:17), and it was necessary for Scripture to include his sister who is both his father's daughter and his mother's daughter, even though she is derived by a fortiori and bears the designation of both of them — to teach that we do not issue warnings from logical inference, as is found in Yevamot in the chapter Keitzad (Yevamot 22a-b). But this baraita is either altered, according to the one who says \"we punish from logical inference\" in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 54a-b), or it is not a resolved version. But the reason for lashes for other unclean animals is because Scripture said regarding the prohibition of the hyrax, because it does not split the hoof, and regarding the pig, because it does not bring up the cud. If so, anything that does not bring up the cud and does split the hoof falls under the name of this prohibition — there is no need for a fortiori argument at all." "<b>that one you may eat</b> — and not an unclean animal. But is this not a prohibition [stated as a positive]? Rather, one transgresses it both as a positive commandment and as a negative commandment. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:3). And so it is in Torat Kohanim (Sifra, chapter 3:1). And the Rav, Rabbi Moshe, said (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot, chapter 2, halakha 3) that it serves to exclude the flesh of a human being: \"that one you may eat\" and not a human; his flesh and his fat [are prohibited] by a positive commandment. But we do not find that our Sages expound it thus. And perhaps he derived this from what we learned there in Torat Kohanim (chapter 4:4): Could it be that even the flesh of walking-on-two [i.e., humans] and the fat of walking-on-two should be subject to a negative commandment regarding their consumption? Scripture says, \"this you shall not eat\" — this is under a negative commandment regarding its consumption, but the flesh of walking-on-two and the fat of walking-on-two are not under a negative commandment regarding their consumption. And the Rav inferred from this that it is not under a negative commandment but it is under a positive commandment, and he derived it from the verse \"that one you may eat.\" But this is not correct, for the Sages explicitly stated (Keritot 21a-b) regarding the blood of walking-on-two and the fat of walking-on-two that there is not even a commandment to distance oneself from them. And if his flesh were prohibited, everything that comes from the impure is impure (Bekhorot 5a-b). And the Sages excluded the blood of creeping things and human blood from the prohibition of blood, and they said (ibid.): the blood of a creeping thing is like its flesh, and one receives lashes for it on account of [it being a] sheretz and not on account of [it being] blood, and they treated it like flesh. But what they said, that they are not under a negative commandment regarding their consumption, means only that they were not restrained from it and they are permitted. But in my opinion this would apply to flesh from a living creature, but a dead one is learned (Avodah Zarah 29a-b) by a gezerah shavah from the broken-necked heifer (eglah arufah), which is prohibited in benefit."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:52.510510306Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}